<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Boboli Gardens</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Boboli Gardens are the park behind the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pitti-palace">Pitti Palace</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, laid out for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-i-de-medici">Cosimo I de' Medici</a> and Eleonora of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> from about 1550 to designs by Niccolò Tribolo and later extended. With their terraces, avenues, grottoes, fountains and statues, they became a model for the formal Italian garden and influenced royal gardens across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. They are part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boboli-gardens.jpg" alt="Boboli Gardens" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jard%C3%ADn_de_B%C3%B3boli,_Florencia,_Italia,_2022-09-19,_DD_26.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
